WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.010
Setting up a Mac for local inference starts with a stable base system.

00:00:04.010 --> 00:00:08.070
macOS version, command line tools, package managers, Python environments,

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:10.500
runtime installation, and model storage all matter.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:14.415
The exact toolchain depends on the runtime, but the operating habit is consistent:

00:00:14.415 --> 00:00:18.495
make installation steps repeatable and avoid turning the daily-use system into an

00:00:18.495 --> 00:00:20.000
undocumented experiment.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.500
Package managers and Python environments are useful,

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:26.090
but they can also create confusion when dependencies are installed globally without a

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:26.500
plan.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:29.500
Keep runtime-specific environments separated where practical.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:34.040
Document which tools are installed, where model files live, which commands start services,

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:36.000
and which ports or interfaces are used.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.500
Small notes save large troubleshooting sessions later.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:42.430
Containers may be useful at a high level for packaging some application dependencies,

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:45.640
but Apple Silicon container workflows have their own platform and performance

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:46.495
considerations.

00:00:46.495 --> 00:00:50.505
Containers do not remove the need to understand storage, permissions, networking,

00:00:50.505 --> 00:00:52.000
and runtime compatibility.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:53.200
For many local LLM users,

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:57.645
a native runtime plus clean documentation is simpler than forcing every experiment into a

00:00:57.645 --> 00:00:58.500
container.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.000
Update discipline is part of setup.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.540
A macOS update, package update, runtime update,

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:05.000
or model manager update can change behavior.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:06.470
Before changing a stable system,

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:09.480
record the current working state and keep a practical rollback path.

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:13.435
The goal is to let experiments change while the base system remains understandable,

00:01:13.435 --> 00:01:16.500
recoverable, and safe to use with sensitive prompts or documents.
